What is a Snapshot in AWS?

A snapshot is a backup/copy of the data stored on an EBS volume.

Think of it like taking a photo of your EBS disk at a particular point in time.

Example:

EC2 Instance
     |
     | attached
     ↓
EBS Volume
[ OS + Application + Data ]
     |
     | Snapshot
     ↓
EBS Snapshot
[ Backup of the volume's data ]

Why do we use snapshots?

Suppose your EC2 has important data:

EBS Volume
----------------
OS
Application
Files
Database data
----------------

You take a snapshot:

EBS Volume  ─────→  Snapshot
                    ↓
                 Backup

If something goes wrong, you can use the snapshot to create a new EBS volume.

Snapshot
   |
   ↓
New EBS Volume
   |
   ↓
Attach to EC2
Important point

A snapshot is not a backup of the entire EC2 instance by itself. It is primarily a point-in-time backup of an EBS volume.
this means
It means a snapshot backs up the EBS volume, not the EC2 machine itself.

Think of it like this:

EC2
 |
 └── EBS Volume
       ├── OS
       ├── Files
       └── Data
             |
             ↓
          Snapshot

The snapshot is a backup of that EBS volume.

So if you delete the EC2:

EC2 ❌ deleted

Snapshot ✅ still exists

For example:

EBS volume = your hard disk
Snapshot = backup/copy of that hard disk
EC2 = computer using that hard disk



