When you create an EC2, the root EBS volume is normally created automatically:

EC2
 │
 └── Root EBS
      └── OS

The root volume has a setting called:

Delete on termination = Yes/No

If it is YES
Terminate EC2
     ↓
EC2 deleted
     ↓
Root EBS deleted ❌
If it is NO
Terminate EC2
     ↓
EC2 deleted
     ↓
Root EBS remains ✅

So if you want the root EBS to remain after terminating the EC2, set:

Delete on termination = No

You can configure this while creating the EC2, under the storage/volume settings.






Even if you already created the EC2 with Delete on termination = Yes, you can change it later to No.

However, there is one important point: the EC2 console does not provide the option to change this for an existing instance. For an existing instance, AWS says to use the AWS CLI (or PowerShell).

For your existing EC2

First find the root device name. It is commonly:

/dev/xvda

or

/dev/sda1

Then use:

aws ec2 modify-instance-attribute \
  --instance-id i-xxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxx \
  --block-device-mappings '[{"DeviceName":"/dev/xvda","Ebs":{"DeleteOnTermination":false}}]'



