What is AWS Elastic Beanstalk?

AWS Elastic Beanstalk is a Platform as a Service (PaaS) that makes it easy to deploy, manage, and scale web applications.
You simply upload your application code, and Elastic Beanstalk automatically provisions and manages the required AWS infrastructure, including:

EC2 instances
Load Balancers
Auto Scaling Groups
Security Groups
CloudWatch monitoring
How it works
Your Application Code
         │
         ▼
Elastic Beanstalk
         │
         ├── EC2 Instances
         ├── Load Balancer
         ├── Auto Scaling
         ├── CloudWatch
         └── Security Groups
Supported Platforms

Elastic Beanstalk supports applications written in:

Java
Python
Node.js
PHP
.NET
Ruby
Go
Docker containers
Key Features
Easy deployment – Upload your code using the AWS Console, CLI, or Git.
Automatic scaling – Adjusts the number of EC2 instances based on traffic.
Load balancing – Distributes incoming requests.
Health monitoring – Monitors application health and infrastructure.
Rolling deployments – Supports rolling, immutable, and blue/green deployment strategies.
Access to infrastructure – You still have full access to the underlying AWS resources.
Typical Deployment Flow
Develop your application.
Package the application (ZIP or Docker image).
Create an Elastic Beanstalk application.
Create an environment (Web Server or Worker).
Deploy the application.
Elastic Beanstalk provisions infrastructure automatically.
Monitor and scale as needed.
Pricing

There is no additional charge for Elastic Beanstalk itself. You only pay for the AWS resources it creates, such as EC2 instances, Elastic Load Balancers, RDS databases (if used), and storage.

When to use Elastic Beanstalk

Choose Elastic Beanstalk if you:

Want to deploy applications quickly.
Don't want to manually manage EC2, Auto Scaling, and Load Balancers.
Need automatic scaling and monitoring.
Are hosting traditional web applications or REST APIs.

If you need maximum infrastructure customization or are building a microservices architecture, services such as ECS, EKS, or AWS Lambda may be more appropriate.

Interview Definition

AWS Elastic Beanstalk is a Platform as a Service (PaaS) that allows developers to deploy, manage, and scale web applications without managing the underlying infrastructure. It automatically handles provisioning, load balancing, auto scaling, monitoring, and application deployment while giving developers access to the underlying AWS resources.

